{
 "id": "2019-liu-vegetation",
 "name": "Vegetation Enclosure in Urban Parks: Shenzhen Litchi Park (Liu & Schroth 2019)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Liu, M., & Schroth, O. (2019). Assessment of Aesthetic Preferences in Relation to Vegetation-Created Enclosure in Chinese Urban Parks: A Case Study of Shenzhen Litchi Park. Sustainability 11:1809. https://doi.org/10.3390/su11061809\n\nHow does planting that blocks the view or blocks movement change how a park feels? Participants see 16 computer-generated scenes of Litchi Park, Shenzhen: four viewpoints (A–D), each in four enclosure versions after Robinson: visually and physically open (VPOO), visually open / physically closed (VPOC), visually closed / physically open (VPCO), and visually and physically closed (VPCC). For each scene they answer the paper's three questions on five-point scales (1 = most positive): pleasantness (coherence), whether the scene is appropriate to bear a lot of functions (complexity), and legibility. The demographic and park-use questions follow the translated questionnaire in the paper's supplement. Original result (188 valid responses): enclosed scenes were less legible; with the view blocked, physically open scenes felt more pleasant; physical enclosure had by far the strongest effect on suitability for many functions.\n\nImages: the 16 scenes printed in Figure 3 (CC BY 4.0), made by the authors with Rhinoceros 3D and Xfrog plant models composited with park photographs in Photoshop (not AI-generated), cut from the figure at about 550 × 360 px.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the original showed each picture three times, once per question (48 pages); here the three questions are on one screen per picture; (2) the supplement does not show which scene number belongs to which enclosure type; we follow Figure 3 order (1 VPOO, 2 VPOC, 3 VPCO, 4 VPCC), and scenes appear in the original A-1 … D-4 order; (3) question 12 is missing from the supplement and is left out; (4) images are lower resolution than in the online survey.",
 "author": "Liu & Schroth",
 "year": "2019",
 "category": "Academic Research",
